     1	---
     2	name: ralph
     3	description: "Convert PRDs to prd.json format for the Ralph autonomous agent system. Use when you have an existing PRD and need to convert it to Ralph's JSON format. Triggers on: convert this prd, turn this into ralph format, create prd.json from this, ralph json."
     4	user-invocable: true
     5	---
     6	
     7	# Ralph PRD Converter
     8	
     9	Converts existing PRDs to the prd.json format that Ralph uses for autonomous execution.
    10	
    11	---
    12	
    13	## The Job
    14	
    15	Take a PRD (markdown file or text) and convert it to `prd.json` in your ralph directory.
    16	
    17	---
    18	
    19	## Output Format
    20	
    21	```json
    22	{
    23	  "project": "[Project Name]",
    24	  "branchName": "ralph/[feature-name-kebab-case]",
    25	  "description": "[Feature description from PRD title/intro]",
    26	  "userStories": [
    27	    {
    28	      "id": "US-001",
    29	      "title": "[Story title]",
    30	      "description": "As a [user], I want [feature] so that [benefit]",
    31	      "acceptanceCriteria": [
    32	        "Criterion 1",
    33	        "Criterion 2",
    34	        "Typecheck passes"
    35	      ],
    36	      "priority": 1,
    37	      "passes": false,
    38	      "notes": ""
    39	    }
    40	  ]
    41	}
    42	```
    43	
    44	---
    45	
    46	## Story Size: The Number One Rule
    47	
    48	**Each story must be completable in ONE Ralph iteration (one context window).**
    49	
    50	Ralph spawns a fresh Amp instance per iteration with no memory of previous work. If a story is too big, the LLM runs out of context before finishing and produces broken code.
    51	
    52	### Right-sized stories
    53	
    54	- Add a database column and migration
    55	- Add a UI component to an existing page
    56	- Update a server action with new logic
    57	- Add a filter dropdown to a list
    58	
    59	### Too big (split these)
    60	
    61	- "Build the entire dashboard" - Split into: schema, queries, UI components, filters
    62	- "Add authentication" - Split into: schema, middleware, login UI, session handling
    63	- "Refactor the API" - Split into one story per endpoint or pattern
    64	
    65	**Rule of thumb:** If you cannot describe the change in 2-3 sentences, it is too big.
    66	
    67	---
    68	
    69	## Story Ordering: Dependencies First
    70	
    71	Stories execute in priority order. Earlier stories must not depend on later ones.
    72	
    73	**Correct order:**
    74	
    75	1. Schema/database changes (migrations)
    76	2. Server actions / backend logic
    77	3. UI components that use the backend
    78	4. Dashboard/summary views that aggregate data
    79	
    80	**Wrong order:**
    81	
    82	1. UI component (depends on schema that does not exist yet)
    83	2. Schema change
    84	
    85	---
    86	
    87	## Acceptance Criteria: Must Be Verifiable
    88	
    89	Each criterion must be something Ralph can CHECK, not something vague.
    90	
    91	### Good criteria (verifiable)
    92	
    93	- "Add `status` column to tasks table with default 'pending'"
    94	- "Filter dropdown has options: All, Active, Completed"
    95	- "Clicking delete shows confirmation dialog"
    96	- "Typecheck passes"
    97	- "Tests pass"
    98	
    99	### Bad criteria (vague)
   100	
   101	- "Works correctly"
   102	- "User can do X easily"
   103	- "Good UX"
   104	- "Handles edge cases"
   105	
   106	### Always include as final criterion
   107	
   108	```
   109	"Typecheck passes"
   110	```
   111	
   112	For stories with testable logic, also include:
   113	
   114	```
   115	"Tests pass"
   116	```
   117	
   118	### For stories that change UI, also include
   119	
   120	```
   121	"Verify in browser using dev-browser skill"
   122	```
   123	
   124	Frontend stories are NOT complete until visually verified. Ralph will use the dev-browser skill to navigate to the page, interact with the UI, and confirm changes work.
   125	
   126	---
   127	
   128	## Conversion Rules
   129	
   130	1. **Each user story becomes one JSON entry**
   131	2. **IDs**: Sequential (US-001, US-002, etc.)
   132	3. **Priority**: Based on dependency order, then document order
   133	4. **All stories**: `passes: false` and empty `notes`
   134	5. **branchName**: Derive from feature name, kebab-case, prefixed with `ralph/`
   135	6. **Always add**: "Typecheck passes" to every story's acceptance criteria
   136	
   137	---
   138	
   139	## Splitting Large PRDs
   140	
   141	If a PRD has big features, split them:
   142	
   143	**Original:**
   144	> "Add user notification system"
   145	
   146	**Split into:**
   147	
   148	1. US-001: Add notifications table to database
   149	2. US-002: Create notification service for sending notifications
   150	3. US-003: Add notification bell icon to header
   151	4. US-004: Create notification dropdown panel
   152	5. US-005: Add mark-as-read functionality
   153	6. US-006: Add notification preferences page
   154	
   155	Each is one focused change that can be completed and verified independently.
   156	
   157	---
   158	
   159	## Example
   160	
   161	**Input PRD:**
   162	
   163	```markdown
   164	# Task Status Feature
   165	
   166	Add ability to mark tasks with different statuses.
   167	
   168	## Requirements
   169	- Toggle between pending/in-progress/done on task list
   170	- Filter list by status
   171	- Show status badge on each task
   172	- Persist status in database
   173	```
   174	
   175	**Output prd.json:**
   176	
   177	```json
   178	{
   179	  "project": "TaskApp",
   180	  "branchName": "ralph/task-status",
   181	  "description": "Task Status Feature - Track task progress with status indicators",
   182	  "userStories": [
   183	    {
   184	      "id": "US-001",
   185	      "title": "Add status field to tasks table",
   186	      "description": "As a developer, I need to store task status in the database.",
   187	      "acceptanceCriteria": [
   188	        "Add status column: 'pending' | 'in_progress' | 'done' (default 'pending')",
   189	        "Generate and run migration successfully",
   190	        "Typecheck passes"
   191	      ],
   192	      "priority": 1,
   193	      "passes": false,
   194	      "notes": ""
   195	    },
   196	    {
   197	      "id": "US-002",
   198	      "title": "Display status badge on task cards",
   199	      "description": "As a user, I want to see task status at a glance.",
   200	      "acceptanceCriteria": [
   201	        "Each task card shows colored status badge",
   202	        "Badge colors: gray=pending, blue=in_progress, green=done",
   203	        "Typecheck passes",
   204	        "Verify in browser using dev-browser skill"
   205	      ],
   206	      "priority": 2,
   207	      "passes": false,
   208	      "notes": ""
   209	    },
   210	    {
   211	      "id": "US-003",
   212	      "title": "Add status toggle to task list rows",
   213	      "description": "As a user, I want to change task status directly from the list.",
   214	      "acceptanceCriteria": [
   215	        "Each row has status dropdown or toggle",
   216	        "Changing status saves immediately",
   217	        "UI updates without page refresh",
   218	        "Typecheck passes",
   219	        "Verify in browser using dev-browser skill"
   220	      ],
   221	      "priority": 3,
   222	      "passes": false,
   223	      "notes": ""
   224	    },
   225	    {
   226	      "id": "US-004",
   227	      "title": "Filter tasks by status",
   228	      "description": "As a user, I want to filter the list to see only certain statuses.",
   229	      "acceptanceCriteria": [
